<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apahida</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apahida is a village near Cluj-Napoca in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, known for princely graves of the late 5th century. Finds made in 1889 and 1968 included gold jewellery, garnet-inlaid fittings and horse harness, buried with members of the Gepid elite after the collapse of Hunnic power. They are among the richest burials of the period in the Carpathian basin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apahida.jpg" alt="Apahida" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gheorghe Tare · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apahida,Cluj-DSCF2074.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
